{
  "summary": "Makes the default combined fee estimator in `estimatesmartfee` fall back to the `block_policy` estimate whenever the mempool estimator is unavailable, returning an error only if the block policy estimate also fails. Changes the mempool estimator to return an error rather than the relay fee floor when the mempool is too sparse to fill block-template percentiles, and clears tracked mined-block stats if loading the mempool from disk fails at startup. Also renames the default estimator parameter in `estimatesmartfee` and the internal enum from \"none\" to \"auto\", keeping \"none\" as an accepted alias.",
  "problem": "When the mempool is sparse or empty (such as after a restart without persistence or when mempool.dat is corrupt), the mempool estimator previously returned the fee floor, causing severe underestimation. In other states where the mempool estimator was not ready (during startup, during mempool loading, or under low block coverage), the combined estimator returned an error instead of falling back to valid block policy estimates.",
  "evidence": [
    "The PR description explains that when the mempool is empty or sparse, returning the fee floor 'can be a significant underestimation, since the now-missing mempool may have held much higher-feerate transactions.'",
    "Commit 697d1b7127 explains that without clearing mined-block stats on failed mempool loads, 'the estimator would keep a window describing a mempool the node no longer has and, once the mempool refilled, serve an estimate built on it.'"
  ],
